"""More everyday entries for chat_knowledge (round 2). Imported for its side effect of extending chat_knowledge.E."""
from .chat_knowledge import entry

# how-to
entry('boil egg|eggs', 'how long soft hard perfect',
      'For boiled eggs, lower them into already boiling water and time them: about 6 minutes for a runny yolk, 8 for jammy, and 10–11 for hard-boiled. '
      'Then put them in cold water for a minute or two; it stops the cooking and makes them easier to peel.')
entry('rice', 'cook how perfect fluffy water',
      'For fluffy rice on the hob: rinse it, then use about 1.5 cups of water per cup of white rice (a little less for jasmine). Bring to the boil, cover, turn to the lowest heat for 12–15 minutes, '
      'then leave it covered off the heat for 5–10 minutes before fluffing with a fork.')
entry('pasta', 'cook how long salt water',
      'Cook pasta in plenty of well-salted boiling water, stirring at the start so it does not stick, and taste it a minute or two before the time on the packet. '
      'Keep a cup of the cooking water: a splash helps the sauce cling.')
entry('tie|tying', 'shoelace knot tie how',
      'For a reliable shoelace knot, make a loop with one lace, wrap the other lace around it, and push it through the gap to form a second loop, then pull both loops tight. '
      'If your knots keep coming undone, you may be tying a "granny knot": try wrapping the second lace the opposite way around.')
entry('stain|stains', 'remove get out coffee wine grease clothes',
      'For most fresh stains: blot (do not rub), rinse from the back with cold water, then work in a little washing-up liquid or laundry detergent and wash as normal. '
      'Avoid hot water and the dryer until the stain is gone, as heat can set it. Grease responds well to washing-up liquid; red wine to cold water and a little salt or detergent.')
entry('apolog|sorry', 'how say friend upset after argument',
      'A good apology is short and specific: say what you did, acknowledge how it affected them, say you are sorry without "but", and mention what you will do differently. '
      'Then give them space to respond; they may need time.')
entry('interview', 'job prepare tip nervous',
      'For a job interview: research the organisation, prepare three or four short stories from your experience (a problem, what you did, the result), practise answering out loud, '
      'and prepare two questions to ask them. Nerves are normal; slowing down and pausing before answering helps a lot.')
entry('presentation|public speak|speech', 'nervous tip prepare give',
      'For a presentation: know your opening and closing lines well, practise out loud a few times, and focus on two or three key points. Slow, deliberate breathing before you start helps with nerves, '
      'and pausing is fine; it feels longer to you than to the audience.')
entry('email', 'write professional polite how start',
      'For a clear email: a specific subject line, one short greeting, your main point or request in the first two sentences, any details after, and a clear next step ("Could you reply by Friday?"). '
      'Keep it short and read it once before sending.')
entry('password', 'strong secure make good',
      'A strong password is long and unique: a passphrase of four or more unrelated words (with a number or symbol) is easier to remember and harder to guess than a short complex one. '
      'Use a different password for each important account, ideally with a password manager, and turn on two-factor authentication.')
entry('battery', 'last longer drain charge phone',
      'To make a phone battery last longer: lower screen brightness, turn off location and background refresh for apps that do not need them, and use battery-saver mode. '
      'For battery health over years, avoid letting it sit at 0% or very hot.')
entry('travel|trip|holiday|vacation', 'pack plan tip first time',
      'For a trip: make a short packing list a few days before, pack outfits that mix and match, keep documents, chargers and medication in your hand luggage, and check the weather and any entry rules. '
      'Leaving a little unplanned time often makes the trip more relaxed.')
entry('garden|grow|vegetable|balcony', 'easy start beginner plant what',
      'Easy things to start with on a balcony or small garden: herbs like basil, mint and parsley; salad leaves and radishes, which grow fast; cherry tomatoes in a sunny spot; and strawberries. '
      'Use pots with drainage holes and good compost, give them as much sun as you can, and water when the top of the soil feels dry.')
entry('paint|repaint', 'room kitchen wall colour color choose',
      'For repainting a room: wash and dry the walls, fill holes, tape edges and protect the floor. Use a primer on fresh plaster or strong colour changes, then two thin coats with a roller, '
      'cutting in the edges with a brush first. For a kitchen, a wipeable finish (eggshell or satin) copes better with splashes. Test colour samples on the wall and look at them in daylight and lamplight.')
entry('read|book', 'recommend good suggest more habit',
      'To read more, keep a book where you would otherwise reach for your phone, aim for just 10 pages a day, and give yourself permission to drop books you are not enjoying. '
      'If you tell me a book or genre you liked, I can suggest what kind of book to look for next.')
entry('language|spanish|french|german|italian|japanese', 'learn new how fast',
      'For learning a language: a little every day beats a lot once a week. Mix vocabulary practice (spaced repetition apps work well) with listening to real speech, '
      'and start speaking early, even badly. Learning the most common few hundred words gets you surprisingly far.')
entry('exercise|workout|fit', 'start begin how often beginner home',
      'To start exercising: pick something you can enjoy, start small (20–30 minutes, two or three times a week), and build slowly. A mix of brisk walking or cycling for the heart and simple strength '
      'exercises (squats, push-ups against a wall, lunges) twice a week covers a lot. Consistency matters more than intensity.')
entry('breakfast', 'healthy quick idea',
      'Quick breakfast ideas: porridge with fruit, yoghurt with nuts and berries, eggs on toast, or wholegrain toast with peanut butter and banana. Some protein and fibre helps keep you full until lunch.')
entry('tea', 'make perfect cup brew how long',
      'For a good cup of black tea, use freshly boiled water and steep for about 3–5 minutes; green tea prefers water just off the boil (around 80 °C) and 2–3 minutes, or it turns bitter.')
entry('coffee', 'make good how brew',
      'For better coffee at home: use freshly ground beans if you can, about 1 heaped tablespoon (7–8 g) per 120–150 ml of water, and water just off the boil. Too bitter usually means over-extracted; too sour means under-extracted.')

# science & how things work
entry('plane|airplane|aeroplane', 'fly how stay air',
      'A plane flies because its wings are shaped and angled so they push air downward as the plane moves forward; pushing air down pushes the wing up. That upward force, lift, '
      'grows with speed, which is why planes need a long run-up. The engines provide the forward push.')
entry('gravity', 'what how work',
      'Gravity is the attraction between masses. Earth is so massive that it pulls everything near it towards its centre, which is why things fall and why we have weight. '
      'The same pull keeps the Moon in orbit around Earth and Earth around the Sun.')
entry('star|stars', 'twinkle why',
      'Stars twinkle because their light passes through moving layers of air at different temperatures, which bend it slightly in changing directions. Planets twinkle less because they appear as tiny discs rather than points.')
entry('sun', 'hot why how shine',
      'The Sun shines because of nuclear fusion in its core: hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together so hard by gravity that they fuse into helium, releasing enormous energy as light and heat.')
entry('ice', 'float why water',
      'Ice floats because it is less dense than liquid water. When water freezes, its molecules lock into an open pattern that takes up more space, so ice weighs less than the same volume of water.')
entry('bread', 'rise yeast why',
      'Yeast eats sugars in the dough and gives off carbon dioxide gas. Kneading builds stretchy gluten that traps the gas in little bubbles, so the dough rises; baking sets the airy structure.')
entry('soap', 'work clean how',
      'Soap molecules have one end that likes water and one end that likes grease. The grease-loving ends grab onto oil and dirt, and the water-loving ends let the rinse water carry it all away.')
entry('vaccine', 'how work',
      'A vaccine shows your immune system a harmless version or piece of a germ, so it can learn to recognise it. If the real germ turns up later, your body recognises it quickly and fights it off before you get very ill.')
entry('microwave', 'how work heat',
      'A microwave oven makes water molecules in food jiggle very quickly using microwaves. That motion is heat, so food containing water warms up.')
entry('fridge|refrigerator', 'how work cold',
      'A fridge moves heat rather than making cold: a fluid evaporates in coils inside, soaking up heat, and a compressor squeezes it so it releases that heat through coils at the back.')
entry('wind', 'cause what why',
      'Wind is air moving from areas of higher pressure to lower pressure. The Sun heats the ground unevenly; warm air rises, leaving lower pressure, and cooler air flows in to fill the gap.')
entry('cloud|clouds|rain', 'form how why come',
      'Clouds form when air rises and cools, so its water vapour condenses onto tiny specks of dust as droplets. When droplets merge and grow too heavy, they fall as rain.')
entry('snow|snowflake', 'form how why',
      'Snowflakes form when water vapour freezes directly onto tiny particles high in cold clouds. The six-sided shape comes from how water molecules link up as they freeze, and every flake grows a little differently as it falls.')
entry('tide|tides', 'cause why',
      'Tides are caused mainly by the Moon\'s gravity pulling slightly harder on the side of Earth facing it, stretching the oceans into two bulges. As Earth rotates, coasts pass through them, giving about two high tides a day.')
entry('salt', 'ice road melt why',
      'Salt lowers the freezing point of water. A little liquid water on the ice dissolves the salt, and that salty water stays liquid below 0 °C, so it keeps melting the ice around it.')
entry('onion', 'cry tears why cut',
      'Cutting an onion breaks its cells and releases a chemical that turns into a gas which irritates your eyes, so they make tears to wash it away. A sharp knife and a cold onion help.')
entry('cat|cats', 'purr why',
      'Cats purr when they are content, but also when stressed or hurt, as a way to soothe themselves. The sound comes from rapid twitching of muscles in the voice box.')
entry('dog|dogs', 'tail wag why',
      'Dogs wag their tails to communicate. A loose, wide wag usually means friendly and relaxed, but a stiff or high, fast wag can mean excitement or tension, so the rest of the body language matters too.')
entry('internet', 'how work',
      'The internet is a huge network of computers passing information in small packets. Each packet carries an address, and routers forward it step by step until it arrives, where the packets are reassembled.')
entry('inflation', 'what why cause',
      'Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, so the same money buys a little less. It tends to happen when spending grows faster than the supply of goods and services, or when costs such as energy rise.')
entry('placebo', 'what effect',
      'The placebo effect is when people feel better after a treatment with no active ingredient, because they expect it to help. Medicines are tested against placebos to show they work better than expectation alone.')
entry('caffeine', 'how work awake why',
      'Caffeine keeps you alert by blocking adenosine, a chemical that builds up in your brain during the day and makes you feel sleepy. Its effect halves roughly every five hours.')
entry('yawn', 'contagious why',
      'Yawning is still debated; ideas include tiredness, cooling the brain and social signalling. Yawns are contagious partly because we unconsciously mirror the people around us.')
